# contributor: GitHub operations for a Dagger coding agent.
#
# The capabilities, smallest first:
#
# - `ghApi` makes read-only GitHub REST requests (GET-only by design).
#
# - `reviewComments` reads pull request review threads via the GitHub API,
#   so an agent can be pointed at review feedback — a PR number, a PR URL,
#   or a single-comment #discussion_r link — and read the full thread with
#   its file/line/diff context.
#
# - `prComment` leaves feedback on a pull request — on the conversation, or
#   anchored to a file/line of the diff. Comments are agent-authored by
#   definition, so the tool enforces a ":robot:" body prefix that tells
#   readers a machine wrote the comment, not the human operator.
#
# - `submitPr` opens a pull request for a branch that is already on the
#   remote. Getting the branch there is the committer module's business
#   (commit, then push); this tool is only the last step, so the whole
#   contribution flow is reviewable primitives instead of one opaque leap.
#
# - `pushBranch` publishes the workspace's committed HEAD to a branch on a
#   DIFFERENT repository — typically a fork, for when the agent cannot or
#   should not push branches to the repository it checked out. Committer's
#   push only reaches the checkout's own remote; this one names its
#   destination, completing the fork flow: checkout upstream → edit →
#   commit → pushBranch(repo: "you/fork", branch: "fix-x") →
#   submitPr(branch: "you:fix-x").
#
# - `checkout` re-points the agent at a DIFFERENT repository: it fetches a
#   git repo as a Workspace (GitRef.asWorkspace, authenticated with the
#   module token so pushes work) and returns a continuation of the current
#   conversation bound to that checkout. The agent keeps its own toolset —
#   tools live on the conversation, and their `Workspace!` args simply
#   auto-fill from the new binding — and the target workspace's agents, if
#   it has any, are composed ON TOP (minus modules the originating
#   workspace already provides). A checked-out workspace refuses export by
#   construction, so the only way its changes leave is commit + push.
#
# These replace the old monolithic `contribute` tool, which predated
# committer's push and the staff module: it fetched the repo, ran a
# throwaway sub-agent to completion, and turned the delta into a PR in one
# opaque call. Now the main agent decides — run the errand itself via
# checkout → edit → commit → push → submitPr, or hire a staff worker to do
# exactly that while its own workspace stays put.
#
# Configuration lives in dagger.toml settings, not per-call arguments:
# `githubToken` authenticates every GitHub operation, reaching the container
# only as a secret env var (GH_TOKEN) — never baked into an image layer,
# never echoed. `remote` names the GitHub repository bare PR numbers resolve
# against; it's a property of the project rather than of any one task (a
# permanent fork would simply configure a different value).

# Tail-truncate command output to keep tool results context-friendly.
let tailLines = 200
# Patches read top-down, so they get head-truncated instead.
let patchLines = 400

# Last `lines` lines of s, with a truncation marker when shortened.
let tail(s: String!, lines: Int! = tailLines): String! {
  let all = s.split("\n")
  if (all.length <= lines) {
    s
  } else {
    let marker = "… (" + toString(all.length - lines) + " earlier lines truncated) …"
    marker + "\n" + all.takeLast(lines).join("\n")
  }
}

# First `lines` lines of s, with a truncation marker when shortened.
let head(s: String!, lines: Int! = patchLines): String! {
  let all = s.split("\n")
  if (all.length <= lines) {
    s
  } else {
    let marker = "… (" + toString(all.length - lines) + " later lines truncated) …"
    all.takeFirst(lines).join("\n") + "\n" + marker
  }
}

"""
GitHub operations: read pull request reviews and the REST API, comment on
pull requests, open pull requests for pushed branches, and switch the
conversation to a checkout of another repository.
"""
type Contributor {
  # Private so the secret is never exposed as a field/tool argument.
  let token: Secret = null
  # The GitHub repository PR numbers resolve against. Private: configured in
  # settings, not steered per-call.
  let remote: String! = ""

  new(
    """
    A GitHub token used to authenticate API calls, push branches, and open
    pull requests.

    Wire it up in dagger.toml settings rather than passing it around:

        [env.dev.modules.contributor.settings]
        githubToken = "env://GITHUB_TOKEN"

    Without it, only public repositories can be checked out, and everything
    that talks to the GitHub API is unavailable.
    """
    githubToken: Secret = null,
    """
    The GitHub repository this project's pull requests live in, as
    "owner/name" (a github.com URL works too). Like the token, this belongs
    in dagger.toml settings — it's a property of the project, not of any one
    task, so it shouldn't vary between contributors or throwaway forks (a
    permanent fork would simply configure a different value):

        [env.dev.modules.contributor.settings]
        remote = "dagger/dagger"
    """
    remote: String! = "",
  ) {
    self.token = githubToken
    self.remote = remote
    self
  }

  """
  Compose the contributor tools onto a base LLM.
  """
  agent(base: LLM!): LLM! @agent {
    base
      .withTools(currentNode)
      .withSystemPrompt(systemPrompt)
  }

  """
  Read the review comments on a GitHub pull request, grouped into threads
  with their file, line, and diff context.

  Accepts a bare PR number (resolved against the configured remote
  repository) or a full PR URL. A link to a single comment — the
  `#discussion_r<id>` URLs GitHub hands out — narrows the output to just
  that comment's thread.
  """
  reviewComments(
    """
    The pull request to read: a number like "13831", a URL like
    "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13831", or a single-comment link
    like "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13831#discussion_r3732814956".
    """
    pr: String!,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): review threads are live conversations — new replies must
    # show up on the next read, not be replayed from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }

    let repo = ""
    let number = ""
    let discussion = ""
    let ref = pr.trimPrefix("#")
    if (ref.containsMatch(`^\d+$`)) {
      repo = remoteSlug
      number = ref
    } else {
      let m = ref.match(`github\.com/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/pull/(\d+)(?:/files)?(?:#(?:discussion_)?r(\d+))?`)
      if (m == null) {
        raise `not a pull request reference: "${pr}" — pass a PR number or a github.com PR URL`
      }
      # Indexing is statically nullable; groups always exist on a match, and
      # the unmatched optional fragment group surfaces as "".
      repo = (m.captures[0] ?? "") + "/" + (m.captures[1] ?? "")
      number = m.captures[2] ?? ""
      discussion = m.captures[3] ?? ""
    }

    # Fetch every review comment on the PR, then let jq group them into
    # threads (replies carry in_reply_to_id pointing at the thread root) and
    # render them — narrowed to one thread when DISCUSSION_ID is set.
    let script = """
      set -eu
      # Secret commands (e.g. `gh auth token`) commonly emit a trailing
      # newline; HTTP headers reject it.
      GH_TOKEN="$(printf '%s' "$GH_TOKEN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
      export GH_TOKEN
      if [ -n "$DISCUSSION_ID" ]; then
        printf '## Review thread discussion_r%s on %s#%s\n\n' "$DISCUSSION_ID" "$OWNER_REPO" "$PR_NUMBER"
      else
        printf '## Review comments on %s#%s\n\n' "$OWNER_REPO" "$PR_NUMBER"
      fi
      comments="$(gh api --paginate "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$PR_NUMBER/comments?per_page=100")"
      printf '%s' "$comments" | jq -rs -f /tmp/render.jq
    """

    let ran = toolbox
      .withNewFile("/tmp/render.jq", renderJq)
      .withEnvVariable("OWNER_REPO", repo)
      .withEnvVariable("PR_NUMBER", number)
      .withEnvVariable("DISCUSSION_ID", discussion)
      # live read: never serve replies from the exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script], expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      raise `reading review comments for ${repo}#${number} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` + "\n\n" + tail(ran.combinedOutput)
    }

    # Threads read top-down, so head-truncate like a patch.
    head(ran.stdout)
  }

  """
  Make an authenticated, read-only GitHub API request (`gh api`) and return
  the response, optionally filtered through a jq expression.

  Covers everything the REST API exposes: code search
  ("search/code?q=repo:owner/name+needle"), PR/issue metadata, releases,
  file listings, and so on. Requests are GET-only — this tool cannot
  mutate anything on GitHub.

  Raw GitHub payloads are huge; pass `jq` to select just the fields you
  need (e.g. ".items[].path").
  """
  ghApi(
    """
    The API path to GET, e.g. "repos/dagger/dagger/releases/latest" or
    "search/code?q=repo:badlogic/pi-mono+toolSnippets". A leading slash is
    fine. Placeholders {owner}/{repo} resolve against the configured remote.
    """
    path: String!,
    """
    jq expression applied to the response (gh's built-in --jq), e.g.
    ".items[] | {path, repository: .repository.full_name}". Empty returns
    the raw JSON.
    """
    jq: String! = "",
    """
    Follow pagination (--paginate) and concatenate all pages. Combine with
    a jq filter to keep the output manageable.
    """
    paginate: Boolean! = false,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): the API is a live read — issues get comments, releases
    # get published; don't replay stale responses from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }

    let script = """
      set -eu
      # Secret commands (e.g. `gh auth token`) commonly emit a trailing
      # newline; HTTP headers reject it.
      GH_TOKEN="$(printf '%s' "$GH_TOKEN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
      export GH_TOKEN
      set -- api --method GET "${API_PATH#/}"
      if [ -n "$PAGINATE" ]; then set -- "$@" --paginate; fi
      if [ -n "$JQ_EXPR" ]; then set -- "$@" --jq "$JQ_EXPR"; fi
      gh "$@"
    """

    let ran = toolbox
      .withEnvVariable("API_PATH", path)
      .withEnvVariable("JQ_EXPR", jq)
      .withEnvVariable("PAGINATE", if (paginate) { "1" } else { "" })
      # live read: never serve API responses from the exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script], expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      raise `GET ${path} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` + "\n\n" + tail(ran.combinedOutput)
    }

    # JSON reads top-down, so head-truncate like a patch.
    head(ran.stdout)
  }

  """
  Leave feedback on a GitHub pull request as a comment — on the PR
  conversation, or anchored to a file (and optionally a line) of the diff.

  Comments posted this way are agent-authored, so `body` MUST start with
  ":robot:" — the marker that tells readers a machine wrote the comment,
  not the human operator. Calls without the prefix are rejected.
  """
  prComment(
    """
    The pull request to comment on: a number like "13831" (resolved against
    the configured remote) or a URL like
    "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13831".
    """
    pr: String!,
    """
    The comment body (Markdown), starting with ":robot:". Keep it kind,
    brief, and to-the-point.
    """
    body: String!,
    """
    File path within the repository to anchor the comment to, as a review
    comment on that file. Empty posts on the PR conversation instead.
    """
    path: String! = "",
    """
    Line number to anchor the comment to, in the file's post-PR version.
    The line must appear in the PR's diff. 0 anchors to the file as a
    whole. Requires `path`.
    """
    line: Int! = 0,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): posting a comment is a side effect, not something to
    # replay from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }
    if (!body.trimSpace.hasPrefix(":robot:")) {
      raise robotPrefixError
    }
    if (line != 0 and path == "") {
      raise "a line anchor needs a file: pass `path` along with `line`"
    }

    # Same PR-reference forms as reviewComments, minus the discussion links
    # (replying into a thread is reviewComments territory; this tool opens
    # new ones).
    let repo = ""
    let number = ""
    let ref = pr.trimPrefix("#")
    if (ref.containsMatch(`^\d+$`)) {
      repo = remoteSlug
      number = ref
    } else {
      let m = ref.match(`github\.com/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/pull/(\d+)`)
      if (m == null) {
        raise `not a pull request reference: "${pr}" — pass a PR number or a github.com PR URL`
      }
      repo = (m.captures[0] ?? "") + "/" + (m.captures[1] ?? "")
      number = m.captures[2] ?? ""
    }

    # File-anchored comments go through the review-comments API (which needs
    # the PR's current head commit); plain feedback is an issue comment on
    # the PR conversation. The body travels as a file, never spliced into
    # shell source.
    let script = """
      set -eu
      # Secret commands (e.g. `gh auth token`) commonly emit a trailing
      # newline; HTTP headers reject it.
      GH_TOKEN="$(printf '%s' "$GH_TOKEN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
      export GH_TOKEN
      if [ -n "$FILE_PATH" ]; then
        head_sha="$(gh api "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$PR_NUMBER" --jq .head.sha)"
        set -- api --method POST "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$PR_NUMBER/comments" \
          -F "body=@/tmp/body.md" \
          -f "commit_id=$head_sha" \
          -f "path=$FILE_PATH"
        if [ "$LINE" -gt 0 ]; then
          set -- "$@" -F "line=$LINE" -f side=RIGHT
        else
          set -- "$@" -f subject_type=file
        fi
        gh "$@" --jq .html_url
      else
        gh api --method POST "repos/$OWNER_REPO/issues/$PR_NUMBER/comments" \
          -F "body=@/tmp/body.md" --jq .html_url
      fi
    """

    let ran = toolbox
      .withNewFile("/tmp/body.md", body)
      .withEnvVariable("OWNER_REPO", repo)
      .withEnvVariable("PR_NUMBER", number)
      .withEnvVariable("FILE_PATH", path)
      .withEnvVariable("LINE", toString(line))
      # posting must actually happen every time, not be served from the
      # exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script], expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      raise `commenting on ${repo}#${number} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` + "\n\n" + tail(ran.combinedOutput)
    }

    `Posted comment: ${ran.stdout.trimSpace}`
  }

  """
  Open a pull request for a branch that is already on the remote.

  This is the last step of a contribution, not the whole trip: make the
  change, commit it, and push it to a branch first (the committer tools) —
  then submit that branch. The PR is opened exactly as described here; review
  your work with the git tools before calling this.
  """
  submitPr(
    """
    The head branch to open the pull request from — the branch you pushed,
    e.g. "fix-span-leak". For a PR from a fork, qualify it as "owner:branch".
    """
    branch: String!,
    """
    Pull request title.
    """
    title: String!,
    """
    Pull request body (Markdown). Say what changed and why, for a reviewer.
    """
    body: String! = "",
    """
    The GitHub repository to open the pull request on, as "owner/name" (a
    github.com URL works too). Empty targets the configured remote — pass
    this explicitly after a `checkout` of another repository.
    """
    repo: String! = "",
    """
    Base branch the pull request merges into. Empty means the repository's
    default branch.
    """
    baseBranch: String! = "",
    """
    Open the pull request as a draft.
    """
    draft: Boolean! = false,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): opening a PR is a side effect, not something to replay
    # from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }
    let slug = if (repo == "") { remoteSlug } else { normalizeSlug(repo) }

    # The body travels as a file, never spliced into shell source.
    let script = """
      set -eu
      # Secret commands (e.g. `gh auth token`) commonly emit a trailing
      # newline; HTTP headers reject it.
      GH_TOKEN="$(printf '%s' "$GH_TOKEN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
      export GH_TOKEN
      set -- pr create --repo "$OWNER_REPO" --head "$BRANCH" \
        --title "$PR_TITLE" --body-file /tmp/pr.md
      if [ -n "$BASE_BRANCH" ]; then set -- "$@" --base "$BASE_BRANCH"; fi
      if [ -n "$DRAFT" ]; then set -- "$@" --draft; fi
      gh "$@"
    """

    let ran = toolbox
      .withNewFile("/tmp/pr.md", body)
      .withEnvVariable("OWNER_REPO", slug)
      .withEnvVariable("BRANCH", branch)
      .withEnvVariable("BASE_BRANCH", baseBranch)
      .withEnvVariable("PR_TITLE", title)
      .withEnvVariable("DRAFT", if (draft) { "1" } else { "" })
      # PR creation must actually happen every time, not be served from the
      # exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script], expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      raise `opening a pull request on ${slug} from ${branch} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` +
        "\n\n" + tail(ran.combinedOutput)
    }

    `Opened pull request: ${ran.stdout.trimSpace}`
  }

  """
  Publish the workspace's committed HEAD to a branch on ANOTHER repository —
  typically a fork, for when you cannot or should not push branches to the
  repository you checked out. The committer `push` tool only reaches the
  checkout's own remote; this one names its destination, so the fork flow is
  checkout upstream → edit → commit → pushBranch → submitPr with branch
  "owner:branch".

  Pending changes are NOT pushed: commit first. Authentication comes from
  the configured GitHub token when present, or the host's own git
  credentials (e.g. SSH agent + pushInsteadOf) otherwise.

  A push the remote rejects (its branch moved past ours) is an error, never
  a takeover. To overwrite anyway, pass `expectedRemoteSHA`: the push then
  succeeds only while the remote branch still points at that sha — a lease,
  like `--force-with-lease` — so use it only after reading what the remote
  moved to, and only when discarding that is the intent.
  """
  pushBranch(
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    The destination GitHub repository, as "owner/name" (a github.com URL
    works too) — typically your fork of the repository you checked out.
    """
    repo: String!,
    """
    Destination branch name on that repository, e.g. "fix-span-leak".
    """
    branch: String!,
    """
    Lease on the destination branch: omit for a plain push (fast-forwards and
    branch creation only), or pass the sha the remote branch is expected to
    point at to force-update it from exactly that state.
    """
    expectedRemoteSHA: String = null,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): pushing is an effect on the remote; a later identical
    # call must push again, not replay the first result.
    if (branch.trimSpace == "") {
      raise "pushBranch: the destination branch must not be empty"
    }
    let slug = normalizeSlug(repo)
    let url = if (slug.containsMatch(`^[^/:]+/[^/]+$`)) {
      "https://github.com/" + slug
    } else {
      repo
    }
    # The first read of the workspace's git state: a workspace with no
    # readable history gets the plain answer, not a push error.
    source.git.head.commitSHA rescue { e: Error =>
      return `This workspace has no readable git history (${plainError(e.message)}); ` +
        "there is nothing to push. Pending edits are still visible with status / diff."
    }
    let result = source.git.head.push(
      to: authenticated(url),
      branch: branch,
      expectedRemoteSHA: expectedRemoteSHA,
    ).{{ref, previousSHA, sha, disposition}} rescue { e: Error =>
      raise `push to ${slug} failed: ${plainError(e.message)}`
    }
    let summary = case (result.disposition) {
      GitPushDisposition.UP_TO_DATE =>
        `${result.ref} on ${slug} is already up to date at ${short(result.sha)}; nothing to push.`
      GitPushDisposition.CREATED =>
        `Pushed ${result.ref} (new branch) to ${slug} at ${short(result.sha)}.`
      GitPushDisposition.FORCED =>
        `Pushed ${result.ref} to ${slug}: forced ${short(result.previousSHA)} -> ${short(result.sha)}.`
      else =>
        `Pushed ${result.ref} to ${slug}: ${short(result.previousSHA)} -> ${short(result.sha)}.`
    }
    let left = source.git.uncommitted.diffStats.{{path}}.length
    let leftNote = if (left > 0) {
      "\n" + `${toString(left)} file(s) remain uncommitted and were not pushed; status shows them.`
    } else {
      ""
    }
    summary + leftNote
  }

  """
  Switch this conversation to a checkout of a DIFFERENT git repository.

  Your workspace is replaced by a fresh checkout of `repo` — every workspace
  tool you already hold (read, edit, status, diff, commit, push, ...) simply
  operates on the new checkout from here on. When the target workspace
  defines agents of its own, they are composed on top of your toolset,
  except modules your originating workspace already provides.

  The switch is one-way for this conversation: your previous workspace —
  pending changes included — is left behind, and there is no tool to switch
  back. So when the errand is a side quest, hand it to a staff worker (its
  workspace is a disposable copy) instead of switching yourself.

  A checked-out workspace cannot export back to the host: the only way work
  leaves it is commit + push (then submitPr). The configured GitHub token
  authenticates the checkout, so pushes to repositories it can write to work.
  """
  checkout(
    llm: LLM!,
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    Git URL of the repository to check out, e.g.
    "https://github.com/dagger/otel-go".
    """
    repo: String!,
    """
    Branch to check out. Empty checks out the repository's default branch.
    """
    branch: String! = "",
    """
    Also compose the target workspace's own agents onto your toolset. Their
    modules' code and prompts come from the target repository — disable this
    when checking out a repository you do not trust.
    """
    composeAgents: Boolean! = true,
  ): LLM! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): resolves a live remote and rebinds the conversation; a
    # later identical call must fetch the repo as it is then.
    let ref = if (branch == "") {
      authenticated(repo).head
    } else {
      authenticated(repo).branch(branch)
    }
    # Force the resolution HERE, inside the tool, so a typo'd URL or branch
    # fails as this call instead of poisoning the continuation.
    let sha = ref.commitSHA rescue { e: Error =>
      raise `cannot check out ${repo}: ${plainError(e.message)}`
    }
    let label = ref.name.trimPrefix("refs/heads/") rescue { e: Error => "HEAD" }
    let where = `${repo}@${label} (${short(sha)})`

    let target = ref.asWorkspace
    let switched = llm.withWorkspace(target)
    if (!composeAgents) {
      print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout.`)
      return switched
    }

    # Redundancy filter: a module name the originating workspace already
    # composes is assumed to provide the same tools — only the target's OWN
    # agents are layered on. (Colliding tool names among what remains get
    # namespaced by the engine, never silently shadowed.)
    let known = source.agents.list.{{name}}.map { a =>
      a.name.split(":", 2).takeFirst.join("")
    }.uniq
    # Reading the target's agents runs the target repo's module code, which
    # can fail for reasons that shouldn't strand the checkout (a broken
    # config, settings that need a host client): fall back to the plain
    # switch.
    let group = target.agents(exclude: known)
    let extras = group.list.{{name}}.map { a => a.name } rescue { e: Error =>
      return unlayered(switched, where, plainError(e.message))
    }
    if (extras.isEmpty) {
      print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout; ` +
        "it contributes no additional agents.")
      return switched
    }
    let layered = group.compose(base: switched)
    # compose is lazy: force the composition HERE, so a failing target module
    # lands in this rescue instead of poisoning the continuation.
    layered.model rescue { e: Error =>
      return unlayered(switched, where, plainError(e.message))
    }
    print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout, ` +
      `and its own agents were composed on top: ${extras.join(", ")}.`)
    layered
  }

  # ---- helpers ------------------------------------------------------------

  # Shared toolbox for GitHub operations: git, gh, and jq.
  let toolbox: Container! {
    container
      .from("alpine:3.20")
      .withExec(["apk", "add", "--no-cache", "git", "github-cli", "jq"])
  }

  # The configured remote normalized to "owner/name". Raises when no remote
  # is configured.
  let remoteSlug: String! {
    if (remote == "") {
      raise noRemoteError
    }
    normalizeSlug(remote)
  }

  # Normalize a repository reference to "owner/name" (full https/ssh
  # github.com URLs work too; anything else passes through).
  let normalizeSlug(ref: String!): String! {
    let m = ref.match(`github\.com[:/]([^/]+)/([^/]+?)(?:\.git)?/?$`)
    if (m == null) {
      ref
    } else {
      (m.captures[0] ?? "") + "/" + (m.captures[1] ?? "")
    }
  }

  # The repository at `url`, carrying the module token when one is configured
  # so private fetches and later pushes authenticate; without one, public
  # repositories still work read-only.
  let authenticated(url: String!): Dagger.GitRepository! {
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      return git(url)
    }
    git(url, httpAuthToken: tok)
  }

  # A checkout with the target's own agents left out: the fallback when
  # reading or composing them fails, which must never strand the switch
  # itself.
  let unlayered(switched: LLM!, where: String!, reason: String!): LLM! {
    print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout; ` +
      `its own agents could not be composed (${reason}).`)
    switched
  }

  # Engine errors carry a "[traceparent:<trace>-<span>]" marker so an agent
  # can go read the failing span's logs. The failures rescued here are
  # ordinary mistakes in a tool argument — a typo'd URL or branch — not
  # engine faults; drop the marker from the text that gets embedded in a
  # friendly message, keep everything the message actually says.
  let plainError(message: String!): String! {
    message.replaceMatches(`\s*\[traceparent:[^\]]*\]`, with: "").trimSpace
  }

  let short(sha: String!): String! {
    sha.split("", 8).takeFirst(7).join("")
  }

  # jq program rendering a PR's review comments (a JSON array of pages, via
  # `jq -s` over `gh api --paginate`) as markdown threads. DISCUSSION_ID
  # narrows the output to the thread containing that comment.
  let renderJq: String! = """
    # A comment anchors at the LAST line of its diff hunk, so keep the tail.
    def trimhunk: split("\n")
      | if length > 24
        then ["… (" + ((length - 24) | tostring) + " earlier hunk lines truncated) …"] + .[-24:]
        else .
        end
      | join("\n");
    (env.DISCUSSION_ID // "") as $did
    | add
    | map(. + {thread: (.in_reply_to_id // .id)})
    | if $did == "" then .
      else
        ( map(select(.id == ($did | tonumber)))
          | if length == 0
            then error("comment r" + $did + " is not a review comment on this pull request")
            else .[0].thread
            end
        ) as $root
        | map(select(.thread == $root))
      end
    | if length == 0 then "No review comments."
      else
        group_by(.thread)
        | map(
            .[0] as $top
            | "### "
              + ($top.path // "(general)")
              + (if $top.line != null then ":" + ($top.line | tostring)
                 elif $top.original_line != null then ":" + ($top.original_line | tostring) + " (outdated)"
                 else ""
                 end)
              + "\n"
              + (if ($top.diff_hunk // "") != ""
                 then "\n```diff\n" + ($top.diff_hunk | trimhunk) + "\n```\n"
                 else ""
                 end)
              + (map("\n"
                     + (.user.login // "ghost")
                     + " (" + .created_at + ") discussion_r" + (.id | tostring) + ":\n\n"
                     + .body + "\n")
                 | join(""))
          )
        | join("\n---\n\n")
      end
  """

  let noTokenError: String! = """
    No GitHub token is configured, so GitHub API operations are unavailable.
    Wire one up in dagger.toml:

        [env.dev.modules.contributor.settings]
        githubToken = "env://GITHUB_TOKEN"
  """

  let robotPrefixError: String! = """
    Comments posted by this tool are agent-authored and must be marked as
    such: start the body with ":robot:" so readers can tell the comment was
    written by a machine, not the human operator.
  """

  let noRemoteError: String! = """
    No GitHub remote is configured, so a bare PR number cannot be resolved.
    Set the repository in dagger.toml (or pass a full PR URL instead):

        [env.dev.modules.contributor.settings]
        remote = "dagger/dagger"
  """

  # Instructions for the agent holding these tools.
  let systemPrompt: String! = """
    You have GitHub tools: `reviewComments` and `ghApi` for reading,
    `prComment` for review feedback, `submitPr` for opening pull requests,
    and `checkout` for switching this conversation to another repository.

    `ghApi` GETs any GitHub REST API path with authentication — code search
    ("search/code?q=repo:owner/name+needle"), PR/issue metadata, releases,
    CI check runs. Reach for it instead of fetching github.com web pages,
    which render as mostly navigation noise. Always pass a `jq` filter to
    keep the response small; raw payloads rarely fit in context.

    `reviewComments` reads the review comment threads on a pull request —
    each with its file, line, and diff context. Pass a PR number (resolved
    against the project's configured GitHub remote) or a full PR URL; the
    single-comment links people paste in conversation (ending in
    #discussion_r<id>) narrow the output to just that comment's thread. When
    asked to look at or address review feedback, read the thread first and
    act on what it actually says.

    `prComment` leaves feedback on a pull request: pass the PR and a body,
    plus optionally a file path (and line within the diff) to anchor it as
    a review comment. Comments posted this way are agent-authored, so the
    body must start with ":robot:" — the tool rejects it otherwise. Keep
    feedback kind, brief, and to-the-point.

    `submitPr` opens a pull request for a branch that is ALREADY on the
    remote — it is the last step, not the whole trip. A contribution is the
    ordinary flow you already know, made of reviewable steps: edit, review
    with the git tools, `commit`, `push` to a feature branch, then
    `submitPr` with a real title and a body written for a reviewer. It
    targets the configured remote unless you pass `repo`.

    `pushBranch` publishes the workspace's committed HEAD to a branch on
    ANOTHER repository — typically a fork, for when you cannot or should
    not push branches to the repository you checked out (the committer
    `push` only reaches the checkout's own remote). The fork flow is:
    checkout upstream → edit → commit → pushBranch(repo: "you/fork",
    branch: "fix-x") → submitPr(branch: "you:fix-x"). Pending changes are
    not pushed: commit first.

    `checkout` replaces this conversation's workspace with a fresh checkout
    of another git repository — for when work belongs somewhere else, e.g.
    an upstream bug in a dependency. Your tools come along and simply
    operate on the new checkout, and any agents the target workspace itself
    defines are composed on top of them. The switch is one-way: your
    previous workspace, pending changes included, is left behind. A checked
    out workspace cannot export back to the host — its work leaves only by
    commit + push — so the full errand is checkout → edit → commit → push
    (or pushBranch, to a fork) → submitPr.

    Because checkout is one-way, prefer delegating the whole errand when you
    have the means to (e.g. a staff worker or sub-agent whose workspace is a
    disposable copy): tell it to checkout the repository, make the change,
    commit, push, and submit the PR, and your own workspace stays bound.
    Only checkout yourself when switching repositories IS the task at hand.
  """
}
